## Changes

**`src/types.ts`** — added `storehouse` to the union:
```ts
export type BuildingKind = "hut" | "farm" | "quarry" | "storehouse";
```

**`src/sim.ts`** — three additions:
1. Cost for the new building:
   ```ts
   storehouse: { wood: 25, stone: 10 },
   ```
2. A prerequisites table (only `storehouse` lists one):
   ```ts
   export const BUILDING_PREREQUISITES: Partial<Record<BuildingKind, BuildingKind>> = {
     storehouse: "hut",
   };
   ```
3. The requested function, filtering every building kind to those that are both affordable (`canAfford`) and prereq-satisfied:
   ```ts
   export function availableBuildings(settlement: Settlement): BuildingKind[] {
     return (Object.keys(BUILDING_COSTS) as BuildingKind[]).filter((kind) => {
       const prerequisite = BUILDING_PREREQUISITES[kind];
       const prereqSatisfied = prerequisite === undefined || settlement.buildings.includes(prerequisite);
       return canAfford(settlement, kind) && prereqSatisfied;
     });
   }
   ```

`hut`/`farm`/`quarry` have no entry in `BUILDING_PREREQUISITES`, so `prerequisite === undefined` makes them always allowed — matching the "no prerequisite" requirement. `storehouse` resolves only when a `hut` is already in `settlement.buildings`.

**`tests/sim.test.ts`** — added a `availableBuildings` describe block covering: storehouse available after a hut + affordable, hidden before a hut, and hidden when unaffordable.

## Verification
- `npm run typecheck` (tsc --noEmit): clean
- `npm test` (vitest run): 7/7 passing

I kept the design data-driven (costs and prerequisites in tables) so adding another prerequisite building later is a one-line table entry rather than a logic change.


